$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0}\dfrac{1-e^{x}}{\ln\left(2-e^{x}\right)}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[1-e^{x}]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[\ln\left(2-e^{x}\right)]}\qquad\gray{\text{L’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{-e^x}{\left(\dfrac{1}{2-e^x}\right)(-e^x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-e^{(0)}}{\left(\dfrac{1}{2-e^{(0)}}\right)(-e^{(0)})}\qquad\gray{\text{Substitution}}
\\\\
&=1
\end{align}$

Note that we were only able to use L’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[1-e^{x}]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[\ln\left(2-e^{x}\right)]}$ actually exists.
